    {
      "id": "1a481649-da77-4450-afd2-1e331a5ab435",
      "from": "af170006-e4c6-441a-af6b-37cb9200f4da",
      "beforeOrder": 3,
      "to": "df881d2e-20d9-515b-a26f-c4af48ba63e4",
      "order": 0,
      "beforeContent": {
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "content": "Due to the optic nerve, each retina has a small region without photoreceptors, resulting in a blind spot in each eye’s visual field. In each eye’s visual field, the blind spot is located roughly 10° temporally (away from the nose) and has a size of approximately 6°<sup class=\"citation-ref\" data-ref=\"8\">8</sup>. The blind spot was first discovered in the 17th century by French physicist and priest Edme Marriott<sup class=\"citation-ref\" data-ref=\"9\">9</sup>. We tend not to notice our blind spots in part because our brain in so good at ‘filling in’ missing visual information. To find your own blind spot, try one of these two methods:"
          },
          {
            "type": "break_section",
            "steps": [
              "Put your monitor an arm’s length away. Close your left eye and fix your right eye’s gaze on the ‘B’. Extend your right hand and block the ‘B’ with the tip of your index fingers. Slowly move your hand directly rightwards, and when it gets about halfway across the screen your fingertip should disappear.",
              "Close your left eye and fix your right eye’s gaze on the ‘B’. Move closer and farther away from the screen until the ‘A’ disappears. Repeat with your left eye while fixating on the ‘A’ and the ‘B’ should disappeared."
            ],
            "title": "Blind spot"
          }
        ]
      },
      "beforeLevel": 1,
      "beforeHeader": false
    },
    {
      "id": "671e3832-e311-406d-821a-97f313f9db95",
