    {
      "id": "1dd1d2ae-f64f-4d19-8ead-f1a343bcae09",
      "from": "fa9a9189-d188-47cf-978a-092813998bbc",
      "beforeOrder": 6,
      "to": "f70d870f-11ad-5dff-acfb-63d8f4409b56",
      "order": 0,
      "beforeContent": {
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "content": "One of the first written accounts of color blindness comes from John Dalton – of atomic theory fame – at the end of the 18th century. He noted that he and his brother were color blind, and he assembled a small list of other color blind people. Most happened to be males. Dalton hypothesized that his vitreous humour was tinted blue, and thus absorbed longer wavelengths<sup class=\"citation-ref\" data-ref=\"69\">69</sup>. He donated his eyes upon his death, but no pigment abnormalities were found with his vitreous humour nor his lens. It is now known that color vision starts with the three different types of cones (blue, green and red, as outlined above). It turns out that about 8% of males are red-green color blind, due to a genetic mutation leading to dysfunction of either red or green cones. Other types of color blindness exist, but are more rare. Genetic analysis in the 1990s of Dalton’s preserved eye tissue revealed that he was missing functional middle wavelength (green) cones<sup class=\"citation-ref\" data-ref=\"69\">69</sup>."
          },
          {
            "type": "break_section",
            "title": "Color blindness"
          }
        ]
      },
      "beforeLevel": 2,
      "beforeHeader": false
    },
